<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s08e03" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 3: Solving Inequalities, Part 2</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 3 Overview</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Solve compound inequalities
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write solutions to linear inequalities in interval notation.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    We solve regular equalities and inequalities by performing the same action on both sides:
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, in
    <md>
      2x=6
    </md>
    we divide both sides by 2:
    <md>
      \frac{2x}{2} = \frac{6}{2}
    </md>
    <md>
      x=3
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    For example, in 
    <md>
      x + 3 \ge 7
    </md>
    We subtract 3 from both sides:
    <md>
      x+3-3 \ge 7 - 3
    </md>
    <md>
      x \ge 4
    </md>
  </p>
  <p>
    When we have a compound inequality, like
    <md>
      3 \le x + 5 \le 7
    </md>
    We can solve the inequality by performing the same action in all three parts of the inequality. 
    To get rid of the added 5 next to <m>x</m>, we can subtract 5 from all three parts.
    <md>
      3-5 \le x + 5 - 5 \le 7 - 5
    </md>
    <md>
      2 \le x \le 7
    </md>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the inequality and write your answer in interval notation.
      <md>
        -7 \le 2x+1 \le 9
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: We start by subtracting 1 from all three parts, so that only variable terms are left in the center:
      <md>
        -7-1 \le 2x+1-1 \le 9-1
      </md>
      <md>
        -8 \le 2x \le 8
      </md>
      Then we divide all three parts by 2, to get rid of the multiplied 2 on the <m>x</m>:
      <md>
        \frac{-8}{2} \le \frac{2x}{2} \le \frac{8}{2}
      </md>
      <md>
        -4 \le x \le 4
      </md>
      This says that <m>x</m> can be between -4 and 4, including -4, but not including 4. In interval notation, that would be
      <md>
        [-4, 4]
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s08e03">
    <title>Season 8, Episode 3 Practice Problems</title>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Solve the inequalities and write the answer in interval notation:
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> -15 \lt 3x \le 9</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> -2 \lt \frac{x}{3}+1 \le 4</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> 5 \lt 3x-4 \le 20</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> 7 \ge 2x-1 \gt -5</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> -10 \le 4-x \le 6</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> -8 \le 5x+2 \lt 12</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> -2 \lt \frac{x}{3}+1 \le 4</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> 1 \le 2x+3 \le 13</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
      <task workspace="1.0in">
        <statement>
          <p>
           <m> -6 \gt 2x-4 \ge -20</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.5in">
      <p>
        	Mona is planning her son’s birthday party and has a budget of $285. 
          The Fun Zone charges $19 per child. How many children can she have at the party and stay within her budget?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.5in">
      <p>
        	A water taxi has a maximum load of 1,800 pounds. If the average weight of one person is 150 pounds, how many people can safely ride in the water taxi?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise workspace="2.5in">
      <p>
        	Adele and Walter found the perfect venue for their wedding reception. The cost is $9,850 for up to 100 guests, plus $38 for each additional guest. 
          How many guests can attend if Adele and Walter want the total cost to be no more than $12,500?
      </p>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section> 